  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA primarily zonal jet stream remains fixed across the northern \nCONUS this weekend into early next week with low amplitude \nshortwaves riding across the top of a flattened ridge centered \nacross the four corners and extending east into the ArkLaTex. \nThrough the week ridging becomes more pronounced as it shifts \neast across the southern plains with the jet becoming more anti-\ncyclonically curved and steering shortwaves more to our north in\nsouthern Saskatchewan and Manitoba decreasing chances for rain \nafter Tuesday. Notably we do not look to warm up with this round\nof ridging so temps will remain in the 70s and low 80s through \nthe next several days for highs.\n\n- Severe risk tonight\n\nGiven the increasing density of CAMS and machine learning all \nhighlighting south central and southeast North Dakota this \nevening there has been a 5% CIG1 introduced for large hail this\nafternoon. Confidence in coverage still precludes any \ncategorical upgrades however. With most models now honing in on \nsomewhere around 2000 MUCAPE this evening and shear in the \nneighborhood of 40-50kts, robust rotating elevated supercells \nseem to be the preferred storm mode if storms form. Weak forcing\ncomes in the form of theta-e advection along a 925-850mb front \nthat will slowly sag south through the night. Sounding analogs \nsupport this conceptual model with multiple events of 2.5-3\" \nhail coming up in the SARS system. Even on a low end the \nexpectation tonight (if storms form) would likely be 1.5-2\" \nhail. Given the expected elevated nature of storms the tornado \nthreat looks to be near zero with wind an some uncertainty but \nperhaps a risk for 60-70mph if a cold pool can establish itself \namid the 900-1200 DCAPE. The main threat overall looks to be \nthe hail though from 8pm to as late as 5am with multiple rounds \nof storms possible.\n\n- Sunday and beyond\n\nWith the active zonal jet numerous shortwaves are likely to rotate \ninto the northern plains with machine learning fixating more on the \nMT/WY/western Dakotas corridor for typical late summer high plains \naction. However, an at least buoyant airmass comprised of daily\nSBCAPE values of 500-1000 j/kg and enough shear for some degree\nof storm organization, and a conditional hail and wind threat \nis likely to persist each day through Tuesday across the western \nand southern portions of the forecast area.",
